            <ArticleTitle>[Spinal cord compression due to tophaceous vertebral gout: a case report and literature review].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>253-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="INTRODUCTION" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Acute gout arthritis and tophaceous gout of the spine is rare.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="EXEGESIS" NlmCategory="METHODS">We report the case of a 54-year-old man with chronic low back pain. Physical examination and myelography showing neurological compression on L4 laminectomy evidenced tophaceous gout.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Gout arthritis should always be suspected and investigated in patients with either chronic low back pain or non-specific spinal cord compression.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
